---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import PercentageCalc from '../components/tools/PercentageCalc';

const faq = [
  { q: "How do I calculate a percentage of a number?", a: "Multiply the number by the percentage and divide by 100. For example, 20% of 150 is (20 × 150) ÷ 100 = 30." },
  { q: "How do I calculate percentage change?", a: "Subtract the old value from the new value, divide by the old value, and multiply by 100. For example, from 80 to 100: (100 − 80) ÷ 80 × 100 = 25% increase." },
  { q: "How do I find what percentage one number is of another?", a: "Divide the part by the whole and multiply by 100. For example, 30 is what percent of 200? Answer: (30 ÷ 200) × 100 = 15%." },
  { q: "What is the difference between percentage and percentile?", a: "A percentage is a portion out of 100 (scoring 85% on a test). A percentile shows where a value ranks in a distribution — being in the 90th percentile means you scored higher than 90% of test-takers." },
  { q: "Why isn't a 50% drop cancelled out by a 50% rise?", a: "Percentage change is relative to the starting value, which shifts. $100 falling 50% is $50; a 50% rise from $50 is only $75, not $100. To fully recover a 50% loss you need a 100% gain." },
  { q: "What are percentage points versus percent?", a: "If a rate goes from 10% to 15%, that's a rise of 5 percentage points but a 50% increase in relative terms. Percentage points measure absolute gaps between percentages; percent measures the proportional change." }
];
---

<ToolLayout title="Percentage Calculator" description="Calculate percentages, percentage change, and percentage difference easily. Free and instant." slug="percentage" faq={faq}>
  <PercentageCalc client:load slot="tool" />

  <Fragment slot="tutorial">
    <h2>How to Use the Percentage Calculator</h2>
    <p>This calculator handles three of the most common percentage questions, each on its own mode. Choose "% of number" to take a portion of a value, "% change" to compare two values over time, or "what %" to express one number as a share of another. Enter the two numbers for whichever mode you pick and the answer appears instantly, with no rounding surprises.</p>

    <h2>The Three Percentage Formulas</h2>
    <p>A percent literally means "per hundred," so every calculation scales by 100. Finding a percentage of a number uses <code>result = (percent ÷ 100) × number</code>, so 15% of 200 is <code>0.15 × 200 = 30</code>. Percentage change uses <code>((new − old) ÷ old) × 100</code>, so a price moving from 80 to 100 is a <code>(20 ÷ 80) × 100 = 25%</code> increase. And "what percent is X of Y" uses <code>(part ÷ whole) × 100</code>, so 42 out of 50 is <code>84%</code>.</p>

    <h2>Percentage Change vs. Percentage Points</h2>
    <p>A common source of confusion is that percentage change is always measured relative to the starting value, which keeps moving. A 50% drop followed by a 50% rise does not return to the original — $100 falls to $50, then rises only to $75. This also explains percentage points: an interest rate going from 10% to 15% is a 5 percentage-point rise, but a 50% relative increase. Keeping the base straight prevents costly misreadings of statistics.</p>

    <h2>Where Percentages Show Up</h2>
    <p>Percentages are the everyday language of proportion. You use them to add sales tax at checkout, work out a restaurant tip, gauge a discount while shopping, read the interest rate on a loan or savings account, track investment returns, and interpret news figures like inflation or poll results. Because the underlying formulas are easy to transpose, this calculator lets you jump between the three modes without re-learning the math each time.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
